rictly valid for plane surfaces, but not for large spherical surfaces.</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is True, Statement 2 is a CORRECT explanation for Statement 1"},{"identifier":"B","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is True, Statement 2 is NOT a CORRECT explanation for Statement 1"},{"identifier":"C","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is False"},{"identifier":"D","content":"Statement 1 is False, Statement 2 is True"}],"correct_options":["C"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<h3><strong>Statement 1</strong></h3>\n<p><em>The formula connecting <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.439ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.397ex\" height=\"1.441ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -443 1501.7 637\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D462\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D462\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(572,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1016.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D463\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D463\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> and <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.244ex\" height=\"2.059ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 550 910\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D453\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D453\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> for a spherical mirror is valid only for mirrors whose sizes are very small compared to their radii of curvature.</em></p>\n<p>✅ <strong>True.</strong>  </p>\n<p>This is due to the <strong>paraxial approximation</strong>—the mirror formula <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -1.108ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"11.364ex\" height=\"3.065ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -864.9 5022.9 1354.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(237.7,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(220,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D453\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D453\"></use></g><rect width=\"588.9\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1106.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(2162.5,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(245.5,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(220,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D462\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D462\"></use></g><rect width=\"604.5\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3229.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\" transform=\"translate(4229.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(220,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(225.3,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D463\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D463\"></use></g><rect width=\"553.6\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> holds only when the mirror aperture is small so that rays make small angles with the principal axis. This avoids spherical aberration.</p>\n\n<h3><strong>Statement 2</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Laws of reflection are strictly valid for plane surfaces, but not for large spherical surfaces.</em></p>\n<p>❌ <strong>False.</strong>  </p>\n<p>The <strong>laws of reflection</strong> (angle of incidence = angle of reflection) are fundamental and apply to all reflecting surfaces—plane, spherical, or irregular. What changes in large spherical mirrors is that reflected rays do not all converge at a single focus due to geometry, but the law of reflection itself <strong>still holds locally</strong> at every point on the surface.</p>\n\n<h3><strong>Conclusion</strong></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Statement 1: <strong>True</strong>  </p></li>\n<li><p>Statement 2: <strong>False</strong></p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>✅ <strong>Correct Option: C</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option C: Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is False.</strong></p>"}},"subject":"physics","subjectGroup":null,"timeAllotted":200000,"topic":"reflection-of-light","type":"mcq","year":2007,"isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"yearKey":"iit-jee-2007-paper-1-offline","active":true},{"question_id":"1lg1yg62t","chapter":"dual-nature-of-radiation","chapterGroup":"modern-physics","country":"in","difficulty":null,"exam":"jee-advanced","examGroup":"jee","languages":["en"],"marks":3,"negMarks":1,"paperId":"iit-jee-2007-paper-1-offline","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2007 Paper 1 Offline","permalink":"pstatement-1p-pif-the-accelerating-potential-in-an-x-jee-advanced-physics-motion-iwlf6lkzqkl2aylz","question":{"en":{"comprehension":null,"direction":null,"content":"<p><b>Statement 1 :</b></p>\n<p>If the accelerating potential in an X-ray tube is increased, the wavelengths of the characteristic X-rays do not change.</p>\n\n<p><b>Statement 2 :</b></p>\n<p>When an electron beam strikes the target in an X-ray tube, part of the kinetic energy is converted into X-ray energy.</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is True, Statement 2 is a CORRECT explanation for Statement 1"},{"identifier":"B","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is True, Statement 2 is NOT a CORRECT explanation for Statement 1"},{"identifier":"C","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is False"},{"identifier":"D","content":"Statement 1 is False, Statement 2 is True"}],"correct_options":["B"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p>Since, Potential increases</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/jaoe38c1mggdcumc/d235f8a3-20a2-4f1b-b007-c95511411adf/a4180c40-a361-11f0-b814-0f72f73fa4cf/file-jaoe38c1mggdcumd.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/jaoe38c1mggdcumc/d235f8a3-20a2-4f1b-b007-c95511411adf/a4180c40-a361-11f0-b814-0f72f73fa4cf/file-jaoe38c1mggdcumd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;max-height:40vh;vertical-align:baseline\" alt=\"IIT-JEE 2007 Paper 1 Offline Physics - Dual Nature of Radiation Question 10 English Explanation 1\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/jaoe38c1mggdcumc/d235f8a3-20a2-4f1b-b007-c95511411adf/a4180c40-a361-11f0-b814-0f72f73fa4cf/file-jaoe38c1mggdcumd.png?format=png 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/jaoe38c1mggdcumc/d235f8a3-20a2-4f1b-b007-c95511411adf/a4180c40-a361-11f0-b814-0f72f73fa4cf/file-jaoe38c1mggdcumd.png?format=png 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/jaoe38c1mggdcumc/d235f8a3-20a2-4f1b-b007-c95511411adf/a4180c40-a361-11f0-b814-0f72f73fa4cf/file-jaoe38c1mggdcumd.png?format=png 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/jaoe38c1mggdcumc/d235f8a3-20a2-4f1b-b007-c95511411adf/a4180c40-a361-11f0-b814-0f72f73fa4cf/file-jaoe38c1mggdcumd.png?format=png 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/jaoe38c1mggdcumc/d235f8a3-20a2-4f1b-b007-c95511411adf/a4180c40-a361-11f0-b814-0f72f73fa4cf/file-jaoe38c1mggdcumd.png?format=png 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/jaoe38c1mggdcumc/d235f8a3-20a2-4f1b-b007-c95511411adf/a4180c40-a361-11f0-b814-0f72f73fa4cf/file-jaoe38c1mggdcumd.png?format=png 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>If we give the constant energy release we observe constant wavelength. So the statement 1 is true.</p>\n<p>From Einstein equation,</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.8ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"15.132ex\" height=\"2.801ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -884.7 6688.5 1238.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(220,394) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"210E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-210E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(576,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(370.6,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D706\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D706\"></use></g><rect width=\"913.5\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1431.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2487,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44A\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3757.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(4757.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D43E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D43E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5646.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"2E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5924.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D438\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D438\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p>W = work function (2.4) eV</p>\n<p>If we given energy above 2.4 eV it will release photon.</p>\n<p>Statement 2 is true.</p>\n<p>Hint : (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.667ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.868ex\" height=\"2.237ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 1709.7 989\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D706\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D706\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(616,-150) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(451,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(980,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D466\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D466\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.364ex\" height=\"1.489ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 603 658\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> target material) does not depend on V<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.375ex;\" xmlns=\"http://w